# SPA fallback, for hosts that read Netlify-style redirect rules
# (Netlify, Cloudflare Pages, and others that adopted the format).
#
# One rule, because one rule is what this needs: anything that is not a real
# file is the app, and React Router resolves the path from there. Static files
# are matched by the host's own filesystem check before any rule here is read,
# so the bundle, the fonts, the audio and the service worker are untouched.
#
# This file ships *inside* the build output, so the fallback travels with the
# app whichever directory is published. The Vercel configuration is the primary
# one and is more precise — it also holds /api/* and /assets/* out of the
# fallback, which this format cannot express without a proxy rule. See
# vercel.json and docs/DEPLOYMENT.md; a backend deployed behind a host that
# reads *this* file would need its own rule added above the catch-all.
/*  /index.html  200
